1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,689
Before we use any software, follow the water.
Rain lands on an area that drains

2
00:00:06,689 --> 00:00:13,582
to the same place—engineers call that area a
catchment. Some water soaks into soil. Some

3
00:00:13,582 --> 00:00:19,614
waits in a shallow basin or rain garden. The
rest runs across the surface and

4
00:00:19,614 --> 00:00:26,978
into pipes. Eventually it reaches an outfall,
simply the point where water leaves the drainage

5
00:00:26,978 --> 00:00:33,167
model. The flow there rises and falls during a
storm. The total area under this

6
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:39,512
curve is event volume: all the water that
passed. The highest point is peak flow:

7
00:00:39,512 --> 00:00:46,406
the highest modeled flow rate during the storm.
A project can lower one without lowering

8
00:00:46,406 --> 00:00:52,516
the other. A different rain pattern can change
both. That is why one plan must

9
00:00:52,516 --> 00:00:58,000
face more than one storm. These drawings are
conceptual, not measured.
